[$pvc <cmd>]: patch version control command line utility.

["NOTICE: currently re-writing, not functional.]

[{:h2 name=about}About]
[*pvc] is a version control system similar to git or mercurial, but is ultra
simple: branches are simply a [$base] followed by a set of unix patches with
incrementing id. The only fundamental disadvantage (or advantage) of using pvc
instead of git is that pvc supports only fast-forward merges, which are the
cleanest and simplest to understand.

[{h2 name=basic}Basic Operation]
The [$pvc] command works very similar to git.

Use [$pvc --help] to get
a full list of available commands. Below are the major ones: [+
* [*init]: initialize your repo. Be sure to edit [$.pvcignore] with
  line-separated lua patterns to ignore, then commit.
* [*commit "description"]: commit the current state, creating a
  patch file in the current branch.
* [*status]: get the current status of your local working tree.
* [*at branch@id]: checkout a revision.
* [*branch "name"]: create a new branch.
* [*cp branch@id paths...]: cherrypick revision. Optionally specific paths.
* [*squash <num>]: squash the top <num> commits from tip.
* [*plan]: swiss-army knife to create editable plan to rebase, reorder,
  split, squash or otherwise modify commits and commit history.
]

[{:h3}pvcignore]
The [$.pvcignore] file should contain a line-separated list of [<#string.find>]
patterns that should be ignored. Items ending in [$/] will apply to whole
directories. A common pvc ignore file might look like:

[$$
# directories
%.git/
%.out/

# extensions
%.so$

# binary files
%./path/to/some_binary
]$

[{:arch h2}Architecture]
As the name implies, pvc is patch-based version control.

A branch is a [$@branch/] dir inside the local [$.pvc/] with a base and a series
of numbered patch files in [$rev/], e.g 01.p, 02.p, etc. A branch also has
a [$tip] file with the current tip id.

Commits are performed by diffing from the previous snapshot, creating the patch
file.

Snapshots are created by applying patch files in either forward/reverse order to
an existing snapshot, with the "zero" snapshot being an empty directory.

Any other modification to a branch (e.g squash, rebase, etc) use a simple state
machine controlled by PVCPLAN ([$pvc plan]) which creates [$.branch] (dot branches)
to store the intermediate state and performs cherry-picks and squashes and finally
either moves the commits (if base=branch) or swaps with the non-dot branch.

Finally, the entire [$.pvc] directory is tracked by a hidden [$@.META] branch
as a single series of patches, allowing the user to (soft) reset to a previous
repo state at any time.

